Along with the Guitar nebula, future observations of the evolution of bow shock nebulae will be possible for the nearby radio-quiet neutron star RX J1856.5-3754 (van Kerkwijk & Kulkarni 2001), only \\sim120 pc away (Kalpan, van Kerkwijk & Anderson 2002; Walter & Lattimer 2002). Even at its relatively low speed (<200 km s{}^{-1}), the neutron star travels \\sim 1^{\\prime\\prime} in a year, and evolution of the nebula should be evident on roughly this time scale. PSR J2124-3358, a nearby millisecond pulsar with a complex bow shock nebula (Gaensler, Jones & Stappers 2002), is also promising in this regard, while the discovery of a bow shock nebula powered by the Poynting flux from a magnetar would allow investigation of the relativistic wind in the presence of an ultra-strong magnetic field.

